  "notes": "\"Hard trap with real chords\" — the conservatory end of the Atlanta bench, and the parameters that carry it are all harmonic. He was formally trained (his father a classical clarinettist), so this model gets genuine voice-leading rather than a vamp: `topVoiceStepwise` true, no `vamp` in `harmonicRhythm` at all, and the two families the research names by name, i–iv–V and i–VI–III–VII, carry the top weights. Seventh weighting is raised over the genre for the inner-voice movement the interviews describe. The drums are the other half: the busiest kick on this bench and a rimshot lane over the layered clap, with `fills.bigEveryBars` at 16 for the 8-bar-boundary crash the research calls out landing on the big structural seams rather than every phrase. ⚠ The two low-weight families (i–iv–v and VI–VII–i) are INFERRED — the research names only the two above, and these exist so the generated chord part reaches its distinct-output floor. They are the first thing to delete if this ever sounds busier than the records. ⚠ `melody.register` sits mid at 67–91 rather than the genre's 72–96 because the research describes mid-register chordal beds with the counter-line above them; the counter-line is where `countermelody.registerOffset` +12 puts it.",
